course: dictionary
lesson: 13974
title: Strong feelings: livid
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show how strong a feeling is. «Cross» and «annoyed» show you are unhappy with something. «Livid» is much stronger. It means so angry you can hardly keep calm. «When Dad found the broken window, he was livid.»
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Dad found a broken window, and nobody is smiling about it. The clues show that livid is about anger, and it is a very big anger. Ask yourself: would a cross person say the same thing? If not, the word is probably stronger.
explain: Livid can also describe skin. «Her arm had a livid bruise» means the bruise was dark purple or blue-grey. Here the word is about colour, not feelings. The sentence tells you what the bruise looks like, so you can use the clues to tell which meaning fits.
fact: Livid means very, very angry, so angry you can hardly keep calm.
fact: «Livid» is much stronger than «cross» or «annoyed».
fact: Livid can describe skin that is dark blue-grey or purple, like a bad bruise.
fact: In old writing, «livid» can also mean pale or ashen, from shock or anger.
fact: Phrases you may meet: «livid with rage», «livid about something», «livid at someone».
exercise: «When Sam saw the broken window, he was livid.» What does livid mean here? | Very sleepy | Happy and excited | *Very, very angry | Hungry for lunch
exercise: «Her arm had a livid bruise.» What colour is the bruise? | Bright red like a tomato | Bright yellow | Pale green | *Dark purple or blue-grey
exercise: Asha says livid is just a fancy word for cross. Why might an author choose livid instead? | It means happy and proud | *It shows the anger is much stronger | It means exactly the same as cross, so there is no reason to choose it | It means a little bit tired
test: «The coach was livid about the late bus.» What does livid tell the reader? | Excited about the trip | Mildly bored | Sorry for everyone | *Furious, far stronger than just cross
test: Which word shows the strongest anger? | *livid | puzzled | annoyed | cross
exam: In an old story, a character is described as «livid» after a fright. What might it mean? | Bright and cheerful | Wet from the rain | *Very pale, like a ghost, from shock | Hungry for dinner
